 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>After 12 years of For Restless Sleepers, Francesca Ruffini asked herself a rather practical question: What would she actually take with her? The answer became a collection conceived as a self-portrait, or perhaps the contents of a very well-edited summer suitcase. “This is my wardrobe,” she said, having gone back through 24 collections and kept only what still felt uniquely hers.</p><p>Ruffini’s disciplined editing was plainly apparent. Ivory, almost half of her own wardrobe, was joined by deep green, blue, and a powdery shirtmaker’s blue, her favorite colors. Pajamas remained the point of departure but were treated with considerably more sartorial intent: On a white version, green twill piping was inlaid rather than simply applied, while a subtly narrowed cuff could be shaped with an internal button. Five tops and two bottoms could be recombined almost like pieces of a personal uniform, including longer trousers that have already migrated from bedtime to evenings out, where Ruffini wears it with a heel.</p><p>The prints underwent the same exercise in reduction. Ruffini mined her archive for fragments: a leaf extracted from a much busier design and enlarged until it became almost monumental; her recurring rose bouquet stripped of its colors and rebuilt in tonal shades; two Hawaiian motifs given just enough gold to keep them from becoming too sober. Of the abundance accumulated over 12 years, only five prints survived. “Clothes are meant to accommodate a woman’s personality rather than supply her with one,” she said.</p><p>There is something almost radical in that degree of consistency. Ruffini has changed proportions over the years, but not her fundamental way of dressing. This collection makes that continuity visible; it’s the wardrobe of someone who has become very good at knowing what to leave behind.</p></div>",
